course: dictionary
lesson: 10313
title: Towpath and the words around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A towpath is a path beside a river or canal. You can work out its meaning from the words around it. «The horse walked along the towpath beside the canal, pulling the boat by a long rope.» The clues are horse, canal, boat and rope. Something that pulls a boat with a rope needs a path beside the water, so a towpath is that path.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «The horse walked along the path beside the canal». It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Read on for more clues. «About 250 years ago Britain dug thousands of kilometres of canals, because a single horse walking on the towpath could pull a boat carrying far more coal than it could pull in a cart on a bumpy road.» The words coal, boat and canals tell you why towpaths mattered long ago.
fact: A towpath is a path along the bank of a river or canal.
fact: Long ago, horses walked on towpaths and pulled barges and boats along with ropes.
fact: Today, towpaths are used for walking, running and cycling.
fact: On a towpath, cyclists should slow down and ring their bells. Keep away from the edge, because the water may be deep and cold.
fact: «Towpath» is a noun. One towpath, two towpaths.
exercise: In «The horse walked along the towpath beside the canal», what is a towpath? | A boat with a long rope | A lock where the water is raised | A road for carts on a bumpy hill | *A path along the bank of a river or canal
exercise: Long ago, why did a horse walk on the towpath? | To carry coal on its back | *To pull a boat along the canal with a rope | To race other horses | To eat the grass on the bank
exercise: Today people use towpaths for walking, running and cycling. Which activity is also a use of a towpath today? | *Walking | Steering a ship out to sea | Pulling barges with horses | Digging a new canal by hand
test: Ella says a towpath is a railway line. Is she right? | Yes, it is a road for cars | No, it is a boat | Yes, because it is a railway | *No, it is a path beside a river or canal
test: Why should cyclists on a towpath slow down and ring their bells? | *To warn walkers and keep everyone safe | To cool the bike down | To make the water go deeper | To race the boats
exam: Why was a horse on a towpath so useful when Britain dug canals? | It could fly over the water | It could stop the boats from moving | *It could pull a boat carrying far more coal than a cart could on a bumpy road | It could carry the coal on its back only
